1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,160
Thanks for watching!

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:58,379
There's an old joke.

3
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:01,840
two elderly women are at a Catskill Mountain resort

4
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:02,980
and one of them says,

5
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:05,140
boy, the food at this place is really terrible.

6
00:01:05,659 --> 00:01:07,900
The other one says, yeah, I know, and such small portions.

7
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,240
Well, that's essentially how I feel about life,

8
00:01:11,719 --> 00:01:15,159
full of loneliness and misery and suffering and unhappiness,

9
00:01:15,379 --> 00:01:17,719
and it's all over much too quickly.

10
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,700
The other important joke for me

11
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:24,060
is one that's usually attributed to Groucho Marx,

12
00:01:24,159 --> 00:01:26,599
but I think it appears originally in Freud's wit

13
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:27,980
and its relation to the unconscious.

14
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:29,740
And it goes like this, I'm paraphrasing.

15
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:32,400
I would never want to belong to any club

16
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:34,040
that would have someone like me for a member.

17
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:37,220
That's the key joke of my adult life

18
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:38,740
in terms of my relationships with women.

19
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:42,020
You know, lately the strangest things

20
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:44,000
have been going through my mind because I turned 40

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,580
and I guess I'm going through a life crisis or something.

22
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:46,940
I don't know.

23
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:48,460
And I'm not worried about aging.

24
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:49,380
I'm not one of those characters.

25
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,580
Although I'm balding slightly on top.

26
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:53,200
That's about the worst you can say about me.

27
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:56,380
I think I'm going to get better as I get older.

28
00:01:56,379 --> 00:02:03,539
You know, I think I'm going to be the balding, virile type, you know, as opposed to, say, the distinguished gray, for instance, you know.

29
00:02:03,839 --> 00:02:04,979
Unless I'm neither of those two.

30
00:02:05,359 --> 00:02:12,379
Unless I'm one of those guys with saliva dribbling out of his mouth who wanders into a cafeteria with a shopping bag screaming about socialism.

31
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:17,199
Annie and I broke up, and I still can't get my mind around that.

32
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:25,479
You know, I keep sifting the pieces of the relationship through my mind and examining my life and trying to figure out where did the screw-up come, you know.

33
00:02:25,479 --> 00:02:30,939
and a year ago we were in love, you know, and I just...

34
00:02:30,939 --> 00:02:32,620
And it's funny, I'm not a morose type.

35
00:02:32,699 --> 00:02:33,699
I'm not a depressive character.

36
00:02:33,919 --> 00:02:37,799
I, you know, I was a reasonably happy kid, I guess.

37
00:02:37,859 --> 00:02:41,079
I was brought up in Brooklyn during World War II.

38
00:02:42,679 --> 00:02:43,780
He's been depressed.

39
00:02:44,099 --> 00:02:45,939
All of a sudden he can't do anything.

40
00:02:46,379 --> 00:02:47,319
Why are you depressed, Alvy?

41
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:49,219
Tell Dr. Flicker.

42
00:02:50,259 --> 00:02:51,560
It's something he read.

43
00:02:52,359 --> 00:02:53,299
Something he read, huh?

44
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,660
The universe is expanding.

45
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:57,400
The universe is expanding?

46
00:02:58,040 --> 00:02:59,580
Well, the universe is everything.

47
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,439
And if it's expanding, someday it will break apart

48
00:03:02,439 --> 00:03:03,980
and that will be the end of everything.

49
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,340
What is that your business?

50
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:08,740
He stopped doing his homework.

51
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:10,220
What's the point?

52
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:12,640
What has the universe got to do with it?

53
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:14,020
You're here in Brooklyn.

54
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:15,820
Brooklyn is not expanding.

55
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,660
It won't be expanding for billions of years yet, Albie.

56
00:03:19,659 --> 00:03:23,879
We've got to try and enjoy ourselves while we're here, huh?

57
00:03:24,259 --> 00:03:24,520
Huh?

58
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:24,599
Huh?

59
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:33,539
My analyst says I exaggerate my childhood memories,

60
00:03:33,819 --> 00:03:36,300
but I swear I was brought up underneath the roller coaster

61
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:37,840
in the Coney Island section of Brooklyn.

62
00:03:38,379 --> 00:03:39,819
Maybe that accounts for my personality,

63
00:03:40,019 --> 00:03:41,659
which is a little nervous, I think.

64
00:03:42,859 --> 00:03:45,280
You know, I have a hyperactive imagination.

65
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,139
My mind tends to jump around a little,

66
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,400
and I have some trouble between fantasy and reality.

67
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:53,580
My father ran the bumper car concession.

68
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,580
There he is, and there I am.

69
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,880
I used to get my aggression out through those cars all the time.

70
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:04,920
I remember the staff at our public school.

71
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:08,640
You know, we had a saying that those who can't do, teach,

72
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:10,780
and those who can't teach, teach Jim.

73
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:14,580
And, of course, those who couldn't do anything, I think,

74
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,040
were assigned to our school.

75
00:04:17,139 --> 00:04:21,939
I must say, I always thought my schoolmates were idiots.

76
00:04:22,319 --> 00:04:24,740
Melvin Greenglass, you know, his fat little face,

77
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:28,039
and Henrietta Farrell, just Miss Perfect all the time,

78
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:29,379
and Ivan Ackerman.

79
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:31,680
Always the wrong answer. Always.

80
00:04:32,279 --> 00:04:33,839
Seven-three is lying.

81
00:04:35,419 --> 00:04:37,279
Even then, I knew they were just jerks.

82
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:40,519
In 1942, I had already discovered women.

83
00:04:43,039 --> 00:04:45,319
No, he kissed me! He kissed me!

84
00:04:45,319 --> 00:04:48,040
That's the second time this month.

85
00:04:48,519 --> 00:04:49,099
Step up here.

86
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:49,860
What did I do?

87
00:04:49,939 --> 00:04:50,759
Step up here.

88
00:04:50,879 --> 00:04:51,719
What did I do?

89
00:04:51,819 --> 00:04:53,519
You should be ashamed of yourself.

90
00:04:53,879 --> 00:04:56,540
Why? I was just expressing a healthy sexual curiosity.

91
00:04:57,060 --> 00:05:00,040
Six-year-old boys don't have girls on their minds.

92
00:05:00,459 --> 00:05:01,040
I did.

93
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:02,399
For God's sake, Sally,

94
00:05:02,699 --> 00:05:04,939
never before speaks of a latency period.

95
00:05:05,339 --> 00:05:07,279
Well, I never had a latency period.

96
00:05:07,539 --> 00:05:08,579
I can't help it.

97
00:05:09,039 --> 00:05:11,120
Why couldn't you have been more like Donald?

98
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:13,860
Now there was a model boy.

99
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:15,960
Tell the folks where you are today, Donald.

100
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:19,199
I run a profitable dress company.

101
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:22,480
Boy, sometimes I wonder where my classmates are today.

102
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:26,939
I'm president of the pinkest plumbing company.

103
00:05:28,259 --> 00:05:29,600
I sell tallies.

104
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:32,340
I used to be a heroin addict.

105
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:34,139
Now I'm a methadone addict.

106
00:05:36,639 --> 00:05:37,879
I'm into leather.

107
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,920
I lost track of most of my old schoolmates,

108
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:43,540
but I wound up a comedian.

109
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:47,660
They did not take me in the army. I was, interestingly enough, I was 4P.

110
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:51,800
Yes. In the event of war, I'm a hostage.

111
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:56,620
You always only saw the worst in people.

112
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:59,759
You never could get along with anyone in school.

113
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:02,600
You were always out of step with the world.

114
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:05,960
Even when you got famous, you still distrusted the world.

115
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:10,020
I distinctly heard it. He muttered under his breath, Jew.

116
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:10,759
You're crazy.

117
00:06:10,759 --> 00:06:13,099
No, I'm not. We were walking off the tennis court,

118
00:06:13,099 --> 00:06:15,099
and, you know, he was there and me and his wife,

119
00:06:15,099 --> 00:06:17,099
and he looked at her and then they both looked at me,

120
00:06:17,099 --> 00:06:19,099
and under his breath he said, Jew.

121
00:06:19,099 --> 00:06:21,099
Alva, you're a total paranoid.

122
00:06:21,099 --> 00:06:24,099
How am I a paranoid? I pick up on those kind of things.

123
00:06:24,099 --> 00:06:26,099
You know, I was having lunch with some guys from NBC,

124
00:06:26,099 --> 00:06:29,099
so I said, uh, did you eat yet or what?

125
00:06:29,099 --> 00:06:31,099
And Tom Christie said, no, Jew.

126
00:06:31,099 --> 00:06:33,099
Not did you. Jew eat? Jew?

127
00:06:33,099 --> 00:06:36,099
No, not did you eat, but Jew eat? Jew?

128
00:06:36,099 --> 00:06:37,099
You get it? Jew eat?

129
00:06:37,099 --> 00:06:39,099
Uh, Max, you, uh...

130
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:39,860
You're calling me Max.

131
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,520
Why, Max? It's a good name for you.

132
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:43,879
Max, you see conspiracies in everything.

133
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,860
No, I don't. You know, I was in a record store.

134
00:06:45,939 --> 00:06:46,420
Listen to this.

135
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:49,980
So I know there's this big, tall, blonde, crew-cutted guy,

136
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,960
and he's looking at me in a funny way and smiling,

137
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:54,600
and he's saying, yes, we have a sale this week on Wagner.

138
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:56,600
Wagner, Max. Wagner.

139
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:59,420
So I know what he's really trying to tell me very significantly.

140
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:00,000
Wagner.

141
00:07:00,939 --> 00:07:01,640
Right, Max.

142
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:03,640
California, Max.

143
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:06,420
Just forget the hell out of this crazy city.

144
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:07,740
Forget it, Max.

145
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:10,680
We moved to sunny L.A. All of show business is out there, Max.

146
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:12,680
No, I cannot. You keep bringing it up,

147
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:15,360
but I don't want to live in a city where the only cultural advantage

148
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:17,720
is that you can make a right turn on a red light.

149
00:07:17,980 --> 00:07:18,960
All right, Max, forget it.

150
00:07:19,079 --> 00:07:20,460
Aren't you going to be late for meeting Annie?

151
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:22,259
I'm going to meet her in Father Beekman.

152
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:23,740
I think I have a few minutes left, right?

153
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:41,680
Are you on television?

154
00:07:44,019 --> 00:07:44,379
No.

155
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:49,400
You know, once in a while, you know, I occasionally...

156
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:49,900
What's your name?

157
00:07:50,819 --> 00:07:52,740
You wouldn't know it. It doesn't matter. What's the difference?

158
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,319
You're on, uh, the, uh, the Johnny Carson, right?

159
00:07:58,199 --> 00:08:00,259
Once in a while, you know, I'm, you know, every now and then...

160
00:08:00,259 --> 00:08:00,439
What's your name?

161
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:04,199
I'm, uh, I'm Robert Redford.

162
00:08:04,379 --> 00:08:05,079
Come on!

163
00:08:05,079 --> 00:08:07,680
Alfie Singer.

164
00:08:07,839 --> 00:08:08,219
It was nice.

165
00:08:08,439 --> 00:08:10,000
Thanks very much for everything.

166
00:08:10,279 --> 00:08:10,779
Hey!

167
00:08:11,519 --> 00:08:11,839
What?

168
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:14,740
This is Alfie Singer.

169
00:08:14,919 --> 00:08:16,259
Fellas, you know...

170
00:08:16,259 --> 00:08:18,699
This guy's on television.

171
00:08:18,740 --> 00:08:19,719
Alfie Singer, right?

172
00:08:19,719 --> 00:08:20,579
Am I right?

173
00:08:20,639 --> 00:08:21,219
Give me a break.

174
00:08:21,279 --> 00:08:23,099
This guy's on television.

175
00:08:23,319 --> 00:08:24,919
I need the large polo mallet.

176
00:08:25,139 --> 00:08:26,060
Who's on television?

177
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:28,519
This guy on a Johnny Carson show.

178
00:08:28,539 --> 00:08:29,219
Fellas, what is this?

179
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:30,240
A meeting of the Teamsters?

180
00:08:30,579 --> 00:08:31,120
What program?

181
00:08:31,579 --> 00:08:32,460
Can I have your autograph?

182
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:34,179
You don't want my autograph.

183
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:34,379
No, I do.

184
00:08:34,379 --> 00:08:36,879
It's for my girlfriend.

185
00:08:36,879 --> 00:08:38,879
Make it out to Ralph.

186
00:08:38,879 --> 00:08:40,379
Your girlfriend's name is Ralph?

187
00:08:40,379 --> 00:08:41,379
It's for my brother.

188
00:08:44,879 --> 00:08:46,379
I'll be singer!

189
00:08:46,379 --> 00:08:47,879
Really, I'll be singing at Disney?

190
00:08:47,879 --> 00:08:49,379
This is I'll be singer!

191
00:08:49,379 --> 00:08:50,879
I'll be singer over here!

192
00:08:50,879 --> 00:08:53,879
It's all right, fellas.

193
00:08:53,879 --> 00:08:54,879
Jesus, what'd you do?

194
00:08:54,879 --> 00:08:56,379
Come by way of the Panama Canal?

195
00:08:56,379 --> 00:08:57,379
I'm in a bad mood, OK?

196
00:08:57,379 --> 00:08:58,379
Bad mood?

197
00:08:58,379 --> 00:08:59,379
I'm standing with the cast of The Godfather.

198
00:08:59,379 --> 00:09:00,879
You're going to have to learn to deal with it.

199
00:09:00,879 --> 00:09:01,879
Deal?

200
00:09:01,879 --> 00:09:03,379
I'm dealing with two guys named Cheech.

201
00:09:03,379 --> 00:09:06,360
Okay, please. I have a headache, all right?

202
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:09,600
You are in a bad mood. You must be getting your period.

203
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:11,919
I'm not getting my period. Jesus.

204
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:15,000
Every time anything out of the ordinary happens, you think that I'm getting my period.

205
00:09:15,279 --> 00:09:17,259
A little latter. I think one of them may have missed it.

206
00:09:18,059 --> 00:09:19,139
Has the picture started yet?

207
00:09:19,179 --> 00:09:20,259
It started two minutes ago.

208
00:09:21,279 --> 00:09:23,659
That's it. Forget it. I can't go in.

209
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:24,320
Two minutes, Albie.

210
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:26,659
No, I'm sorry. I can't do it. We've blown it already.

211
00:09:26,779 --> 00:09:28,799
You know, I can't go in in the middle.

212
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:31,799
In the middle? We only missed the titles. They're in Swedish.

213
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,680
You want to get coffee for two hours or something?

214
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:36,100
Two hours? No, I'm going in.

215
00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:36,980
Go ahead. Goodbye.

216
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:40,000
While we're talking, we could be inside. You know that?

217
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,880
Hey, can we not stand here and argue in front of everybody?

218
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:43,100
All right.

219
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:43,780
Because I get embarrassed.

220
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:45,600
All right, all right. So what do you want to do?

221
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:47,940
I don't know now. You want to go to another movie?

222
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:50,120
Let's go see The Sorrow and the Pity.

223
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:51,480
Oh, come on. We've seen it.

224
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:53,800
I'm not in the mood to see a four-hour documentary on Nazis.

225
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:56,040
Well, I'm sorry. I can't.

226
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:59,220
I've got to see a picture exactly from the start to the finish.

227
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:00,320
Because I'm anal.

228
00:10:00,320 --> 00:10:03,320
That's a polite word for what you are.

229
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:07,320
We saw the Fellini film last Tuesday.

230
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:09,320
It is not one of his best.

231
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:11,320
He likes a cohesive structure.

232
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:15,320
You get the feeling that he's not absolutely sure what it is he wants to say.

233
00:10:15,320 --> 00:10:20,320
Of course, I've always felt he was essentially a technical filmmaker.

234
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:23,320
Granted, L'Estrada was a great film.

235
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:26,320
Great in its use of negative imagery more than anything else.

236
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:28,320
But that central cohesive core...

237
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:30,320
You know, that must lead to an artist's work,

238
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:31,320
leading from one to the other.

239
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:33,320
He's screaming his opinions in my ear.

240
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:34,320
You know what I'm talking about?

241
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:37,320
Like all that Juliet of the Spirits or Satyricon.

242
00:10:37,320 --> 00:10:40,320
I found it incredibly indulgent.

243
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:41,320
You know, he really is.

244
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:43,320
He's one of the most indulgent filmmakers.

245
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:44,320
He really is.

246
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:46,320
And without getting...

247
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:47,320
Let's put it this way.

248
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:48,320
What are you depressed about?

249
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:51,320
I miss my therapy. I overslept.

250
00:10:51,320 --> 00:10:53,320
What can you possibly oversleep?

251
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:55,320
The alarm clock.

252
00:10:55,320 --> 00:10:57,320
Do you know what a hostile gesture that is to me?

253
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:00,320
I know, because of our sexual problem, right?

254
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:02,320
Everybody online at the New Yorker

255
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:04,320
has to know our rate of intercourse.

256
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,320
It's like Samuel Beckett.

257
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:10,320
I admire the technique, but it doesn't hit me on a gut level.

258
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:12,320
I'd like to hit this guy on a gut level.

